The Ghost Whisperer line has long been a favorite among the ultralight crowd—few down insulated vests and jackets have been able to match the performance-to-weight ratio of this model.

This vest was limited to men's versions only for several years. Fortunately, it has recently been re-released in a women's version. We're looking forward to testing this and will report back.

You can read about why we like the jacket version in our in-depth review of the Mountain Hardwear Ghost Whisperer/2 or our Best Down Jackets article, where it is our ultralight winner.

If an ultralight down vest is what you’re looking for, you could consider the men’s version if you’re able to find a good fit. Or, there are still a handful of women’s vests that can be purchased online. If you can find this vest, we recommend it as a good lightweight option. And we hope that in future seasons Mountain Hardwear will bring the women’s model back. The last thing we need is fewer technical options for women’s-specific gear!

Who needs an insulated vest?

Puffy vests are useful pieces of gear for taking the chill off without needing a full jacket. They can also be layered over other insulating pieces, like fleece jackets, and under more protective pieces like rain jackets. A puffer vest is more compressible and packable than a fleece vest, making it better suited for travel and outdoor adventure.

If you’re heading out into bitterly cold conditions, a vest probably isn’t the best option, and you’ll want to reach for your parka. But for times when it’s chilly or cold, but not quite frigid, a vest can be the perfect companion to keep your core warm. Ultimately, whether a vest is right for you will come down to personal preference.

When to wear an insulated vest

Insulated vests shine in that tricky in-between weather when it’s too cold for just a shirt or fleece but too warm for a full jacket. They’re perfect for keeping your core warm while allowing your arms to move freely—ideal for hiking, layering, or just getting things done around town on cool days.

You’ll reach for a down or synthetic vest most often in shoulder seasons—spring and fall—when mornings are frosty and afternoons warm up fast. They’re also great as a midlayer in winter, worn under a shell or ski jacket for extra core insulation on the slopes or at camp.

The sleeveless design can reduce overheating compared with a fully insulated jacket during high-output activities like hiking, snowshoeing, or chopping wood, while still providing enough warmth to keep your torso comfortable.

Because vests don’t cover your arms, they’re best suited for dry, cool to cold conditions. In wet or windy weather, wear one under a shell jacket for protection. Around town, they layer easily over hoodies, flannels, or sweaters, offering lightweight warmth without bulk.

Whether you’re out on a crisp fall hike, walking the dog early in the morning, or heading out for a winter road trip, an insulated vest is one of the most versatile pieces you can own. It bridges the gap between seasons, adds warmth where it matters most, and fits seamlessly into most layering systems.

What to look for in a down vest

Type of insulation

Down insulation is made with duck or goose down feathers. Down is known for its superior warmth-to-weight ratio, since the natural feathers offer so much loft, which is what makes the garments warmer by creating dead air space to trap heat. For these reasons, down is an excellent insulation material.

The downsides to down are that it’s more expensive than synthetic insulation. Additionally, when untreated down gets wet, it loses its loft, thus causing it to also lose its warmth. Water-resistant shell materials and hydrophobic coatings on the down feathers themselves are all ways to help down perform better in wet conditions.

Synthetic insulation is typically made with polyester or other similar petroleum-based materials. Synthetic insulation can still be very warm, but it usually does not have the same warmth-to-weight ratio as down, and synthetic garments often do not pack as small as down ones. However, puffer vests made with synthetic insulation are typically more affordable than down vests. Unlike down, when most synthetic insulation materials get wet they still maintain their loft and therefore their warmth. Additionally, if you’re looking for an insulation option that’s vegan, synthetic insulation is a good choice.

A lot of outdoor gear now uses a combination of these two insulation materials, usually in separate and specific compartments of the garment. For example, a puffer vest might have down insulation in most of the body, but synthetic insulation in the shoulders because that’s the area most likely to get wet from precipitation.

Warmth

The level of warmth a puffer vest provides is an important consideration, and warmer doesn’t always necessarily mean better. If you know you’ll be relaxing at camp or hanging in town in cold weather, then the loftiest option is probably the best. But if you want something more versatile for use across seasons, or something that moves and breathes well while being active, you probably don’t want the warmest vest on the market. The fill-power of the down, and even more so the amount of insulation used to stuff the baffles of a puffer vest, will affect how warm it is.

Weather resistance

Insulated down and synthetic puffer vests are generally not designed or intended to be fully wind or waterproof like hardshell waterproof jackets. Instead, their primary purpose is to provide warmth. Still, a good vest will be made with an outer shell material that sheds at least a minor amount of moisture or precipitation. This can be achieved both by the weaving of the shell fabric (higher-denier shells are more durable and weather resistant than ones made with smaller thread diameters).

Another important factor is whether that shell material is treated with a durable water repellent (DWR). This will shed light moisture and precipitation (though it will wet out in a downpour or steady rain or snow).

Fill power

The fill power of down essentially refers to the quality of the feathers. The higher the fill power, the higher the quality, which means better loft and more warmth. Higher fill-power feathers are larger than lower ones, which makes them loftier and allows them to create more dead airspace and warmth. The highest fill power on a vest we considered is the Feathered Friends Eos Down Vest, which has a 900-fill down.

Breathability

If you’re looking for a puffer vest to wear while active then how breathable the shell material and vest is overall will be an important factor to consider. Typically, the warmer and more weather resistant a vest is, the less breathable it will be.

Intended use

When deciding which puffer vest is right for you, think about what you primarily intend to use your vest for. If warmth is your top priority, you may have to spend more and the vest will probably not be as light or breathable as others. And if you’re dealing with particularly cold conditions or if maximum warmth is crucial for you, consider an insulated jacket instead. If you prioritize weight savings for backpacking and other backcountry adventures, you may need to compromise on features. Nail down your preferred features and pick a puffer vest that meets those.

Shell fabric

There are a handful of different types of shell fabrics used on insulated vests, from ripstop treated with water repellent to shells that offer stretch. Consider the denier (the size of the threads the fabric is woven with), water repellency, and other factors when selecting a vest that’s ideal for you.

Sustainability

One of the primary factors we consider when testing gear, including these vests, is how sustainably made that piece of gear is. It’s important to factor in durability, since that will help the garment last longer, keeping it out of landfills. We also look for things like responsibly sourced and produced materials. Examples include responsible down or water repellents that avoid the use of PFCs that are harmful to the environment. Many vests use shell material made of recycled material.

We also look for companies that support fair working conditions for the people making their clothing and gear, including vests that are Fair Trade Certified sewn.

Additionally, we value companies with good warranty and repair options. Outdoor gear and clothing is expensive to make, especially when done right. So typically, you’ll need to spend a little more to get a vest (or other gear) that’s made with sustainability in mind, but we think that’s worth it, given that you usually get a higher quality garment that’s better for you, the earth, and the people who make it.

Durability

Durability is a crucial factor to consider with any outdoor gear or apparel. We will rarely recommend a piece of gear or apparel that lacks durability (except in the instance of unique pieces of ultralight gear). Durability not only makes gear more sustainable, it also helps you spend less money and acquire less stuff in the long run.

We believe all of the puffer vests in this review offer great durability, and some stand out more than others. Durability and weight can sometimes have an inverse relationship, so if you’re hard on gear, it’s perhaps best not to go for the lightest on the market. And, like sustainably made gear, quality gear that lasts for many seasons will often cost more than gear that is not well made.

Size and fit

Always be sure to check the fit and sizing chart, or try on in a store, to avoid unnecessary returns. If you want to be able to put lots of layers under your vest, consider sizing up. A puffer vest should fit loose enough to allow for layering while maintaining the loft in the vest. When a down vest fits too snug, the down is constricted without enough room to fully loft, which in turn causes it to be less warm. But, if you want a vest to wear over minimal layers for aerobic activities, it’s better to go with your true size. With a women’s vest in particular, make sure that it is cut to not become too tight around the waist, hips, and chest.

Weight

Do you plan to use the puffer vest for mostly casual wear, or are you looking for a vest that you can stuff into your pack for long backcountry trips? For casual wear, weight is not as important, though it’s still a factor worth considering. If you want the vest for backcountry adventures, weight savings should be a primary factor.

Compressibility

In general, down insulation is more compressible than synthetic insulation. So if you want your puffer vest to pack very small, whether for travel or an outdoor adventure, you may want to consider a down vest over a synthetic one. A down or synthetic puffer vest will generally compress down better than a fleece vest or vest made of other material.

Pockets

All of the vests in this review have at least two hand pockets, though some have additional chest and internal pockets. Consider how much you want to be able to carry and how important pockets are to you and factor that in when deciding on a vest that’s right for you. A hand pocket is a handy place to store a phone for easy access and to prevent its battery from getting too cold to function.

Hood

Since puffer vests are often meant to be lighter, more minimal garments, many of them don’t have hoods, though some do. If the additional warmth is important to you, consider a vest with a hood. But if you’d like a vest that’s lighter and more packable, avoid the hood to save weight.

Price and value

Not all puffer vests are equal, and that’s especially true with price. Some are designed for fashion and some for function. If budget is your top priority, there are great options out there, but there are a handful of features you may have to sacrifice. Typically, when a vest costs more, it’s because better materials are used and/or the vest is made more sustainably than its less expensive counterparts.

Care and maintenance

First, follow the garment’s care label as down vests can be fragile and every model has different care instructions.

A well-cared-for down vest can last for years of adventures. With a little maintenance, you can keep the insulation lofty, the fabric water-repellent, and the fit comfortable season after season. See step-by-step instructions How to Clean and Repair Down Jackets as the guidance is the same as for down vests.

Washing

Most down and synthetic vests can be machine washed on a gentle cycle with cold water and a mild detergent. Skip bleach and fabric softeners—they can damage both the shell fabric and insulation. For down vests, use a down-specific detergent to clean without stripping the natural oils from the feathers. Use a down-safe detergent or a mild, non-detergent soap on a gentle, cold-water cycle.

Drying

Dry your vest on low heat with a few clean tennis balls or dryer balls to help restore loft and break up clumps of insulation. It can take several drying cycles for down to fully fluff up, so be patient and check periodically. Make sure the vest is completely dry before storing it to prevent mildew.

Restoring water repellency

If water stops beading on the surface, it’s time to refresh the DWR (durable water-repellent) finish. Apply a spray-on treatment while the vest is damp, then dry the vest as directed by its care label. Some spray-on treatments need to be tumbled dry on low heat to help it bond to the fabric.

Storage

Avoid long-term compression. Store your vest loosely—either hanging or laid flat—so the insulation stays lofty. A breathable cotton or mesh bag works better than the stuff sack that came with it.

Repairs

For small rips or snags, use Tenacious Tape or NOSO patches to prevent feathers from escaping. Many brands also offer repair services for more significant damage, extending the life of your vest and reducing waste.

Taking a few minutes each season to clean and care for your vest ensures it stays just as warm and reliable as the day you bought it.

FAQ

What do you wear under a down vest?

It depends on the weather and activity. For hiking or other high-output movement, wear a lightweight base layer underneath to avoid overheating. Around camp or in cold weather, add a fleece or light midlayer for extra warmth. Because down vests aren’t waterproof, pair them with a shell or rain jacket in wet conditions—or wear them under a ski jacket for added insulation on cold winter days.

Should a down vest fit tight or loose?

A down vest should fit snug but not tight. It needs enough room for the down to fully loft and trap warm air, but not so much space that cold air seeps in. If you plan to layer underneath, choose a slightly roomier fit; for wearing over a base layer, a trimmer cut keeps warmth close to your core.

What's the difference between down and synthetic insulation?

Down insulation (made from goose or duck feathers) is lighter, warmer for its weight, and more compressible—perfect for packability and dry, cold conditions. Its main drawbacks are cost, delicacy, and loss of warmth when wet.

Synthetic insulation is typically heavier and bulkier but stays warm when damp, dries faster, and is easier to care for. It’s also more affordable and vegan-friendly, making it a great choice for wet or unpredictable weather.

You can read more in Which is better: a down or synthetic jacket?

Are insulated vests warm enough for extreme cold?

Not on their own. Insulated vests keep your core warm but leave your arms exposed, so they work best as a layering piece in very cold weather. For extreme cold, pair a vest under a parka or insulated jacket for maximum warmth.

Are insulated vests easy to pack?

Yes—especially down vests. Most compress into a small stuff sack or their own pocket, making them easy to stash in a backpack or travel bag. Synthetic vests pack slightly larger but still offer excellent warmth for their weight.

What temperatures are best for wearing a puffer vest?

Puffer vests are versatile across seasons. They’re ideal for cool to cold conditions, such as early fall hikes, chilly camp mornings, or layered under a shell on subfreezing days. They work well anywhere you need warmth without the bulk of full sleeves.

Why do bankers wear vests?

The primary reason why bankers wear puffer vests, according to Marketplace, is because they offer a more casual and comfortable look that still maintains a certain level of professionalism. Working in a bank does not require the technical apparel features that most of these vests offer, but perhaps it helps them dream of far-off places while working under the fluorescent lights.

How We Researched and Tested

We researched popular women’s insulated vests and compared product details, media coverage, and customer feedback before deciding which models to test.

We tested the vests over several months on hikes, camping trips, multi-day backpacking trips, and everyday outings. Much of our testing took place in Colorado’s high country, including around Breckenridge, with additional use at elevation in Idaho and Wyoming. Conditions ranged from cool, dry late-summer weather to drizzly evenings, colder nights at camp, and winter and spring layering.

We wore the vests while hiking, backpacking, foraging, traveling, and spending time around town. We evaluated warmth, fit, comfort, durability, weather resistance, packability, pockets, and how well each vest worked with other layers.

We also compared the vests side by side for warmth and water resistance. Throughout testing, we paid attention to how the insulation and shell fabrics performed in real conditions and which vests were the most practical to reach for across different activities and seasons.

